// Measuring a string.

// The font is monospaced, so a string's width is arithmetic and never a walk of
// the glyphs. It must agree with what txt_draw_sc actually advances, or a centred
// label is off by however far the two disagree -- which is why both compute
// `n * cell * sc / 1000` in that order.
txt_width_sc(string s, int sc) {
  int slen = len(s);
  int cell = txt_cell();
  int width = slen * cell * sc / 1000;
} return int width;

// At the UI scale, because txt_draw is: a width that disagreed with what is drawn
// would centre and right-align every label wrongly the moment the window changed
// size.
txt_width(string s) {
  int ui = txt_ui();
  int sc_arg = ui * 1000;
  int width = txt_width_sc(s, sc_arg);
} return int width;

// A power of ten and a per-mille scale, packed into one int: the power in the
// high half, the scale in the low. txt_num_run carries five parameters already
// and a block holds three actions, so the alternative to packing is another
// function in the hottest text loop in the engine.
txt_num_pw(int pw, int sc) {
  int packed = pw * 65536 + sc;
} return int packed;
